ion(s).\nL8: \nL9: Log into MySpiral to:\nL10: \nL11:   * Deposit doctoral thesis\nL12:   * Access master's dissertations\nL13: \nL14: The role of CO2 capture and utilization in mitigating climate change\nL15: \nL16: File(s)\nL17: \nL18: 【1†Mac Dowell Fennell Shah and Maitland NCC 2017 Accepted.pdf (901.1 KB)】\nL19: \nL20: Accepted version\nL21: \nL22: Show more\nL23: \nL24: Author(s)\nL25: \nL26: Mac Dowell, N\nL27: \nL28: Fennell, PS\nL29: \nL30: Shah, N\nL31: \nL32: Maitland, GC\nL33: \nL34: Type\nL35: \nL36: Journal Article\nL37: \nL38: Abstract\nL39: To offset the cost associated with CO2 capture and storage (CCS), there is growing interest in finding commercially viable end-use opportunities for the captured CO2. In this Perspective, we discuss the potential contribution of carbon capture and utilization (CCU).\nL40: Owing to the scale and rate of CO2 production compared to that of utilization allowing long-term sequestration, it is highly improbable the chemical conversion of CO2 will account for more than 1% of the mitigation challenge, and even a scaled-up enhanced oil recovery (EOR)-CCS industry will likely only account for 4–8%.\nL41: Therefore, whilst CO2-EOR may be an important economic incentive for some early CCS projects, CCU may prove to be a costly distraction, financially and politically, from the real task of mitigation.\nL42: Show more\nL43: \nL44: Date Issued\nL45: \nL46: 2017-04-05\nL47: \nL48: Date Acceptance\nL49: \nL50: 2017-02-08\nL51: \nL52: Citation\nL53: \nL54: Nature Climate Change, 2017, 7 (4), pp.243-249\nL55: \nL56: URI\nL57: \nL58: 【2†http://hdl.handle.net/10044/1/62882†hdl.handle.net】\nL59: \nL60: DOI\nL61: \nL62: 【3†https://www.dx.doi.org/10.1038/nclimate3231†www.dx.doi.org】\nL63: \nL64: ISSN\nL65: \nL66: 1758-678X\nL67: \nL68: Start Page\nL69: \nL70: 243\nL71: \nL72: End Page\nL73: \nL74: 249\nL75: \nL76: Journal / Book Title\nL77: \nL78: Nature Climate Change\nL79: \nL80: Volume\nL81: \nL82: 7\nL83: \nL84: Issue\nL85: \nL86: 4\